# Last-handled neat-core version.
#
# Ockham consumes neat-core via an unpinned `path` dependency that tracks head.
# CI (`scripts/check-neat-core-version.sh`) fails when the sibling neat-core
# presents a breaking bump above this baseline — pre-1.0 a higher minor, or any
# higher major — forcing a deliberate upgrade. Clear the gate by updating
# `ockham` for the breaking change and bumping this version to match neat-core.
#
# 0.10.6: bootstrap against the current family pin (NEAT-AI-core #577 synapse
# uniqueness by (fromUUID, toUUID, type)). Ockham issue #1 does not yet mutate
# creatures; the pin documents the sibling we compile against.
#
# 0.10.6 -> 0.13.0: acknowledge neat-core #633 (Issue #625, 0.12.0), which made
# every `CompiledNetwork` field private behind borrow-only accessors so safe
# code can no longer edit a validated network into an out-of-bounds SIMD read,
# and 0.13.0 (#637, the security-scan milestone roll-up; nothing Ockham names
# changed). Ockham read `num_inputs` and `activations` directly in `stats.rs`;
# those reads now go through `num_inputs()` and `activations()`. Unhandled, the
# same bump took the GRQ fleet down through rust_scorer (GRQ#4724); Ockham was
# next in line because GRQ builds it from the same sibling neat-core at head.
# Verified by `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` and the full test
# suite against the sibling clone at 0.13.0 (59eb6e6).
0.13.0
